{
  "dom_id": "step-q104960",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14255260/assets/f54ba1417f9307980c2546def29e1ce94f53b1f0e24c311687b3f19f4cbfeb20.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\nI. There exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=8$\n\nII. $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y$\n\nIII. $f$ is a **onto**",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$  in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the diagram, we have $f(2)=8$ (we have an arrow from $2\\in X$ to $8\\in Y$). So, there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=8.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the diagram, for all $y$ contained within $Y,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
